#[doc("""
Turning an interrupt into a cancellation, so an interrupted run cleans up
after itself.

A kalku leads its own process group, which is what lets a kill reach the
runtime a launcher started. The cost is that an interrupt no longer
reaches a kalku: Ctrl-C goes to the terminal's foreground group, and a
kalku is not in it. Left alone, that would make the interrupt kill the one
process that knows how to kill the others, and every kalku would be left
holding the reni's build lock — the hang this whole mechanism exists to
prevent.

So the signal is trapped and sent on as `Cancel`, the message a run
already understands: dispatching stops, what was measured is kept, and the
scope unwinds through the ward, which kills each group on the way out. An
interrupted run leaves nothing behind, which is the same promise as a run
that ends.

`SigTerm` as well as `SigInt`, because a supervisor stopping a server
sends the first and a person at a terminal the second, and both have to
leave the project as they found it.
""")]

import kalku.orchestrator.kalku_proc

#[doc("""
Trap the signals that mean *stop* and turn the first of them into
`Cancel`.

Blocks until one arrives, so it belongs in a fiber of its own; a run that
ends first simply leaves it waiting. One signal is enough: a second one
arriving while the run unwinds would be asking the cleanup to abandon
itself.
""")]
pub fn as_cancellation(run: Pid[OrchMsg]) : Unit / Signal + Actor[OrchMsg] {
  Signal.on(SigInt)
  Signal.on(SigTerm)
  let _which = Signal.await()
  Actor.send(run, Cancel)
}
